# Rocdown language reference

Rocdown is Markdown with explicit document-root declarations. Ordinary prose,
email addresses, handles, and fenced code retain their Markdown meaning.

## Declaration recognition

A reserved declaration is recognized only when it starts at the document root,
after optional indentation, and its header matches the expected shape. Reserved
names inside lists, block quotes, or fences stay Markdown.

Use `\@roc` for a literal line-start declaration example outside a fence.

## Declarations

| Form | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `@page { ... }` | Route, draft, layout, theme, color scheme, and metadata |
| `@roc { ... }` | Roc imports, types, and values |
| `@render { expr }` | Insert one Roc `Html` expression |
| `@component` | Declare a Rocci component |
| `@fixture` | Tag preview or test data |
| `@css { ... }` | Add file-scoped CSS |
| `@context`, `@init`, `@on` | Declare a standalone server app |
| `@if`, `@for`, `@match`, `@let` | Insert document-root Rocci template regions |
| `<Tag />` | Instantiate a document-root Rocci component or element |
| `@docs <kind> { ... }` | Bounded documentation component; see below |
| `@img { ... }` | Native image element; `alt` or `decorative` required; see below |

`@island` is reserved in the design but not parsed yet.

## `@img` native images

Use `@img` for first-class image elements with compile-time field extraction and optional sizing. Meaningful images require `alt`. Set `decorative: Bool.true` for purely decorative images (`alt=""`). A non-empty `alt` together with `decorative: Bool.true` is an error. Markdown `![](path)` remains the empty-alt decorative shorthand.

Local `src` paths, including `./img/photo.png`, resolve against the source file
directory. Site-root paths like `/assets/photo.png` and remote `http(s):` /
`data:` URLs pass through. Missing local files are errors in `rocdown run` and in
Rocdown catalog checks. Standalone preview copies those files without hashing and
serves them beside the page route; static site builds hash files under `build.assets`.

````rocdown
@img {
    src: "./img/yammi_banana.png",
    alt: "A delicious banana",
    width: "50px",
    loading: "lazy",
    decoding: "async",
}
````

| Field | Requirement | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `src` | **Required** | Compile-time string literal path or URL |
| `alt` | Required unless `decorative` | Accessible description |
| `decorative` | Optional | `Bool.true` emits empty `alt` for purely decorative images |
| `title` | Optional | Tooltip text |
| `width` | Optional | Explicit width string (e.g. `"50px"`, `"100%"`) |
| `height` | Optional | Explicit height string (e.g. `"50px"`, `"auto"`) |
| `class` | Optional | Custom class name appended to `.rd-image` |
| `loading` | Optional | `"lazy"` or `"eager"` |
| `decoding` | Optional | `"async"`, `"auto"`, or `"sync"` |

## `@docs` components

`@docs` is one reserved family. Kinds such as `note` or `tabs` are not
top-level `@` names. The parser stores the kind identifier and brace body.
Rocdown types the body; standalone `rocdown run` paints a conservative preview.

````rocdown
@docs note {
    title: "Deprecation"

    This API will be removed in 0.4. See [Routing](/reference/rocdown-site/).
}
````

Nested `@docs` are legal in the body. `@page`, `@roc`, `@render`, `@component`,
handlers, and HTML islands inside a docs body are errors. `\@docs` stays prose.

| Kind | Role |
| --- | --- |
| `note`, `tip`, `caution`, `danger`, `deprecated` | Labeled asides |
| `details` | Disclosure with required `summary` |
| `steps` / `step` | Ordered procedure; `step` only inside `steps` |
| `figure` | Exactly one image; nested `@img.alt` owns accessibility; `caption` and `credit` optional; figure-level `alt` is unknown |
| `definition` | Local term and definition |
| `badge` | Block status label |
| `compatibility` | Table of supported combinations |
| `card-grid` / `link-card` | Related-page cards; `page` is a stable page id |
| `file-tree` | Nested unordered list |
| `tabs` / `tab` | No-JS stacked panels; `kind` is `language`, `platform`, or `tool` |
| `include` | File or region include |
| `example` | Display sample with optional `test` metadata |
| `api-operation` | Reserved; Rocdown reports `RD2406` (not shipped) |

See [Write documentation components](/guides/docs-components/) for a working
page. Includes, snippet roots, and `rocdown test` are documented in
[Rocdown site](/reference/rocdown-site/).

## Page metadata

````rocdown
@page {
    route: "/guides/rocdown/",
    draft: Bool.false,
    theme: "paper",
    color_scheme: "auto",
    meta: {
        title: "Rocdown",
        description: "Markdown with explicit Roc and Rocci regions",
    },
}
````

| Field | Constraint |
| --- | --- |
| `route` | Absolute string path; no `..`, query, fragment, encoded separator, or NUL |
| `layout` | Statically resolvable Roc value path |
| `draft` | `Bool.true` or `Bool.false` |
| `theme` | Built-in ID, local theme ID, `none`, or CSS file path |
| `color_scheme` | `auto`, `light`, or `dark` |
| `meta` | Roc record; title and description are statically extracted |

## Markdown profile

Rocdown uses CommonMark with GFM tables, strikethrough, task lists, extended
URL autolinks, and footnotes. It adds deterministic heading IDs, stable `rd-*`
classes, language classes on fenced code, wiki links, and page-link resolution.

Raw HTML in Markdown is disabled by default. Use Markdown, a document-root
Rocci tag, or `@render` instead.

## Footnotes

`[^label]` in ordinary Rocdown is a footnote reference. Pair it with a
definition:

````rocdown
The claim is sourced.[^note]

[^note]: Evidence stays with the document.
````

Definitions are collected out of the article flow into a footnotes section.
Standalone lowering and site HTML share the same accessible contract:
`data-footnote-ref`, `aria-label="Footnotes"`, `data-footnote-backref`, and
stable `fn-{name}` / `fnref-{name}` ids. Missing definitions and duplicate
labels are errors at the reference or definition span. This is not an OKF
`sources[].id` citation.

## Links

Sibling preview supports `[[Page]]`, `[[Page|label]]`, Rocdown file paths, and
same-page heading validation. Absolute routes are checked when a page index is
available. External schemes such as `https:` and `mailto:` pass through.

In static site builds, a passing catalog check rewrites validated page and heading links to
the emitted route (`./foo.rocdown` becomes `/foo/`, and `{id}#heading` becomes
`/route/#heading`). Same-page `#heading` stays a fragment. Asset hrefs are
hashed in a later plan pass. Standalone `rocdown run` still rewrites sibling
stems when `resolve_links` is true.

## Generated values

Every document exports:

```text
rocci_meta    : page metadata record
rocci_content : {} -> Html
rocci_page    : {} -> Html
```

If no GET handler exists for the page route, lowering synthesizes one. A
non-root page also gets a root preview route so `rocdown run` can open it.

The default standalone shell is a single-document reader. When the selected
theme is not `none`, `rocci_page` emits a left `<nav class="rd-toc">` from
heading levels 2 and 3, linking to the same heading IDs used in the article.
H1 is the page title and stays out of the navigator. Empty outlines are
omitted. Theme chrome keeps the article measure, animates navigator jumps
quickly, and hides the navigator on narrow screens and in print. This is not the site
outline: site builds keep a right-hand “On this page” rail inside
documentation-site chrome.

A custom `@page.layout` replaces that shell and does not receive the
automatic navigator.

## Static documentation site support

Rocdown site builds currently accept the static Markdown tree plus `@page` and `@docs`.
They reject dynamic Roc, Rocci templates, `@css`, handlers, and custom layouts until the
island splice path is implemented. Draft pages are excluded from production builds.
